 "content": {
  "subject": "Electrical Machines",
  "topic": "Torque-Slip Characteristics of Three-Phase Induction Motors",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "hook": "Can adding resistance actually make your motor start stronger?",
  "question": "In a slip ring induction motor, what condition yields maximum starting torque?",
  "options": [
   "Rotor resistance equals standstill rotor reactance",
   "Rotor resistance equals zero",
   "Rotor resistance equals synchronous reactance",
   "Rotor reactance equals slip times rotor resistance"
  ],
  "correctIndex": 0,
  "answerLine": "Maximum starting torque occurs when rotor resistance equals standstill rotor reactance.",
  "explanation": [
   "At standstill, the motor has not started turning yet, meaning the operational slip equals exactly one.",
   "The general condition for maximum torque at any operating slip requires rotor resistance to equal slip times reactance.",
   "Substituting a slip value of one into this fundamental relationship simplifies the condition directly to rotor resistance equals reactance.",
   "At starting, rotor current lags heavily behind induced electromotive force due to high standstill inductive rotor reactance."
  ],
  "funFact": "Once the motor accelerates up to rated speed, the external starting resistors are smoothly cut out to maintain high operating efficiency.",
  "outro": "Mastering torque-slip relations guarantees easy marks in State AE exams, so subscribe for more daily engineering walkthroughs!",
